# Copy this file to .env.local for local development.
# In Vercel, set every one of these as a Project → Settings → Environment
# Variable instead — never commit real values.

# --- Database (Neon or Supabase Postgres connection string) -------------
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require"

# --- NextAuth --------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
NEXTAUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-long-random-string"
# The full site URL, no trailing slash. In Vercel this can be set to
# https://your-project.vercel.app or your custom domain.
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# --- Discord OAuth app (Discord Developer Portal → your application) ------
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=""
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# The Goatem Studios Discord server's ID. Login is rejected for anyone who
# isn't currently a member of this guild. This is the ONLY thing Discord
# OAuth controls — it's a login gate, not a permission source. What
# someone can actually DO is decided entirely inside the app: see
# src/lib/permissions.ts, the Ranks page (per-rank actions), and the
# Admin Access page (full access, CREATOR-only).
DISCORD_GUILD_ID=""

# --- The CREATOR ----------------------------------------------------------
# YOUR Discord user ID (Developer Mode on → right-click yourself → Copy
# User ID). This one account always has full access to everything, and is
# the only one who can designate or remove portal admins.
#
# IMPORTANT when setting this in Vercel (or any hosting dashboard): paste
# the bare number only — 792404715912298506, NOT "792404715912298506".
# The quotes below are .env file syntax and get stripped when this file
# loads; a dashboard stores exactly what you type, quotes included. (The
# app now strips stray quotes defensively, but don't rely on that.)
#
# Deliberately an environment variable rather than a database row or a
# Discord role: nothing inside the app — and nobody with any level of
# Discord permission — can grant, revoke, or transfer it. Changing who
# the CREATOR is means editing this value in the hosting dashboard and
# redeploying, nothing else. Leave it blank and NOBODY is CREATOR (it
# fails closed), which also means nobody can appoint admins, so don't.
PORTAL_CREATOR_DISCORD_ID=""

# --- Roblox group (for the roster's GAME ACCESS column) --------------------
# The numeric ID of your Roblox group — the number in the group's URL:
# roblox.com/groups/<THIS NUMBER>/whatever. Bare number, no quotes.
#
# Used to check whether a linked Roblox account is in the group, which is
# what GAME ACCESS reports. Leave it blank and that column stays "unknown"
# for everyone (deliberately not "no access" — see src/lib/roblox.ts).
# No API key needed; the endpoints used are public.
ROBLOX_GROUP_ID=""

# --- Discord bot (REQUIRED for roster sync; also avatars + role colors) ----
# A bot token for the SAME Discord Application as above (Developer Portal →
# your application → Bot → Reset Token). The bot must be invited to your
# guild (OAuth2 → URL Generator → check "bot" scope, minimal permissions is
# fine) for this to work. Powers real avatars and Discord role colors
# throughout the app (see src/lib/discordBot.ts) — everything degrades
# gracefully to placeholders if this is left blank.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=""

# --- Audit log mirror (optional but strongly recommended) -----------------
# A Discord webhook URL (Server Settings → Integrations → Webhooks) for a
# private, staff-only channel. Every audited action gets posted here too,
# so there's a copy of the trail nobody inside the app can edit or clear.
# Leave blank to disable — the database audit log still works without it.
AUDIT_WEBHOOK_URL=""
